a couple years ago i was able to raise  two million dollars for my startup  all because of a single tweet there are  plenty of entrepreneurs who can raise  money quickly because they already know  all of the big vcs  but this was cold outreach to an  investor that i'd never met before  but i knew this would work because i was  using a three-step framework that is  pretty much guaranteed to get you a  meeting with a vc  if executed properly fundraising is  often confusing and frustrating for  early-stage entrepreneurs  but it doesn't have to be follow the  steps outlined in this video  and you'll be having pitch meetings with  plenty of venture capitalists in no time  i've raised money 10 different times now  and even though each time was slightly  different  every successful fundraise follows this  same fundamental pattern  today i'm going to tell you how i raised  two million dollars for my company using  twitter  it's a pretty funny story actually  here's the tweet that brought in two  million dollars  if it looks underwhelming that's because  it was designed to be  a venture capitalist had tweeted about  some research that they were doing  and i simply replied saying that they  missed one important piece of the puzzle  and i could help them even though that  tweet is only 17 words  there's actually a lot going on there so  let's break it down and work through  what you need to do as an entrepreneur  to win the attention of venture  capitalists  everyone thinks that they need to be a  massive influencer in order to leverage  platforms like twitter to raise money  but that's just not true  most entrepreneurs are far too busy to  grow a serious following on social media  and in an odd twist growing a massive  following can actually hurt your  credibility  there's a great saying that goes the  rhetorical style of any twitter account  that continues to gain  followers converges on that of a fortune  cookie  and i've certainly seen this in practice  endless growth requires  endlessly broadening your appeal but  that leads to a kind of lowest common  denominator type of content that anyone  can understand  entrepreneurs need to be experts in  their fields and if you're working on  something truly new and disruptive  there's a good chance that the industry  is extremely small consider crypto at  the early stage  most of the conversation about bitcoin  was hosted on small forums and  completely inaccessible to general  audiences  you had to have strong software  engineering chops to even follow basic  discussions but that was actually a  positive signal to investors so instead  of becoming a fortune cookie account  and constantly tweeting variations of  work hard and anything's possible  i've always focused on talking about  complex topics  where i can actually push the  conversation forward even though those  tweets aren't likely to go  viral anytime soon at the time that i  posted that tweet  i barely had a thousand followers but  you could quickly skim my profile  and know that i had experience in  consumer startups and that was enough to  pass a quick  check by someone visiting my profile and  wondering is this person legit  so before you fundraise it's important  to really know your industry inside and  out  you don't need to be creating loads of  content but if you're going to use a  particular platform  it's good to establish a solid track  record and that's step one  become an expert and people will want to  hear what you have to say  there's a great saying that goes if you  want to be interesting  be interested when you're working on a  new idea  you should be voraciously consuming  information related to that idea  sometimes this means taking classes on  the subject but other times it's just  taking the time to read books  blog posts and technical papers to  really understand where the cutting edge  is  in your industry once you know your  industry code and are ready to start  networking with venture capitalists it's  important to focus on finding founder vc  fit  a lot of entrepreneurs get caught up  chasing celebrity investors  but that's actually a huge mistake for  two main reasons  the first is obvious famous investors  are flooded with pitches all day long  so it'll be hard to break through the  noise but the second reason  is actually more important here famous  investors who don't know about your  market  or aren't interested in it are less  likely to be helpful  it can be tempting to just look at the  midas list of top venture capitalists  and think  everyone on there would be an incredible  addition to your company but a vc who is  focused on biotech  may not be able to help at all with a  crypto project  and vice versa you need to focus on  finding investors who have specific  experience in your industry  look at who's funding companies that are  like yours and then start to follow and  engage with these folks instead of just  aiming for whoever has the biggest name  that's exactly what i did when i was  raising money i stumbled upon someone  who worked at graycroft partners  who was hyper focused on  direct-to-consumer e-commerce companies  his name was teddy citron and he had  only been in venture for a few years  he has a sharp eye for e-commerce  companies and new trends but he's not  exactly a household name in silicon  valley at least not yet  that didn't matter though because i was  focused on founder vc  fit not strictly big names and this is  step  two engage with investors who are  actually experts in your industry when  you find investors who are  active in your industry it can be  tempting to try and pitch them  immediately but coming on too strong can  often be a mistake  i always think of that scene in star  wars where han solo is flying past darth  vader's ship  don't get jittery luke there are a lot  of command chips keep your distance  though chewy but don't look like you're  trying to keep your distance  i don't know fly casual you don't want  to look desperate for funding  but you also don't want to look like  you're hiding the fact that you're  desperate for funding  just fly casual and start having actual  conversations with vcs about what's  going on in your industry so that's what  i did i started engaging with teddy on  twitter  first by reposting some research that  he'd done on the industry he had  essentially collected  every sub-sector of the  direct-to-consumer ecommerce space  and organized them into a massive google  sheet his post had done well on medium  and he certainly didn't need me to  signal boost it but this little shout  out  put me on his radar my tweet only got  three likes  but one of those was from him five weeks  later i followed up  by replying to his original post about  the spreadsheet saying  love this post there's one category you  missed though  happy to chat about it if you're  interested teddy had clearly spent hours  pouring over industry data  and he definitely thought that he had  considered every possible segment but i  knew that he had missed one critical  area  and it happened to be the industry that  i was currently trying to disrupt big  tobacco my co-founders and i  have been grinding on our new idea  called lucy for over a year at this  point and we knew a lot about the  tobacco industry  cigarettes kill millions every year but  the tobacco industry still generates  over one  trillion dollars per year a trillion  with a t  teddy had outlined dozens of micro  industries with market sizes as small as  four billion dollars  but he had completely missed tobacco  there was a good reason for this  no startups had really attacked big  tobacco before so there wasn't much buzz  in silicon valley about the opportunity  that was exactly why we liked the idea  so much  smoking was a huge problem and solving  it would clearly have a positive impact  while still being a good business  now i could have just said hey you  missed tobacco you should add that to  your sheet  but that strategy would have failed  building intrigue  is one of the most powerful  communication skills i try and do it in  every youtube video i make  first by getting you the viewer  interested in what i'm about to discuss  before diving into the details i knew  that anyone who had spent that much time  researching e-commerce companies  wouldn't be able to resist the  opportunity to learn about a new area  that they hadn't considered before  this is step three build intrigue around  what you're working on  so the investor wants to learn more  there's a reason why murder mysteries  are so popular  just open up netflix and you'll see  dozens of shows about mysteries  humans are hardwired to seek out answers  to unsolved questions  it's basic human psychology but i didn't  just leave him hanging  i offered a clear solution a way to make  his analysis more complete  and learn about the missing category  just chat with me  adding a clear call to action there is  critical venture capitalists get flooded  with requests for meetings all day long  and their schedules are packed with  back-to-back calls the easiest way to  get a meeting  is to offer something of value in return  setting time aside for a meeting creates  a cost  so you need to offset that with a  benefit every vc knows that most  meetings  won't result in a good deal these  meetings are risks that they take in  order to find the next great company as  an entrepreneur  you can offset that risk by guaranteeing  that they will learn something  interesting that they didn't know before  even if the investor winds up passing on  the deal  they leave knowing more than they did at  the start of the meeting  and that's valuable to someone whose job  it is to understand markets  my plan here worked perfectly i had  established myself as someone  knowledgeable in e-commerce  found an investor who was uniquely  interested in e-commerce  and then piqued their interest and  offered to help them expand their  understanding of e-commerce  following these three steps will make  cold outreach to investors way more  likely to succeed  investors get flooded with pitch decks  to their emails all day long  so you need to stand out building  relationships slowly and then leveraging  human psychology to ensure that you get  a meeting  is vastly superior to spamming investors  and hoping that they respond to you  when you start fundraising for your  business it's useful to think about the  process like a funnel  at the top of the funnel you have all  the investors who could possibly fund  your business  but you need to narrow that down to just  the investors who would be a good fit  once you have  that list you need to get meetings with  them it's impossible to get an  investment out of every single meeting  even incredible companies like facebook  and google had meetings where investors  passed on the opportunity to invest  so your job is to make sure that when  you reach out to an investor  they take the meeting following this  three-step plan will make sure that you  get those meetings  and don't waste your time reaching out  to dozens of investors who just ignore  you  now to be fair when i sent this tweet i  already had a pretty significant startup  experience under my belt  i'd co-founded soylent and raised over  75 million dollars for that business  but i really think that doesn't matter  all that much  when i was still in college i used the  exact same technique to get in touch  with jim brayer who had led the series a  in facebook and i'd seen giving a talk  at stanford alongside mark zuckerberg in  2012  brayer and his fund excel partners were  riding high off of the facebook  investment  but i was still able to chat with him on  twitter just by following his work  and sending him the occasional  thoughtful message it was super  inspiring at the time  and a big reason why i felt that  starting a company in silicon valley  would be a great move for me  so regardless of your background if you  can become a true  expert in your industry investors will  want to talk to you  it's really that simple so get out there  and start pitching but don't  look like you're pitching you know pitch  casually if you want to talk about  fundraising strategy for your startup  just leave me a comment below or dm me  on twitter and please check out this  recommended video  the youtube algorithm thinks that you'll  really like it

